---
/**
 * ProcessSummary.astro — chapter 5's summary Slide (wayfinder #71 comp F,
 * copy locked by #92, built by #103).
 *
 * The one Slide both routes pass through, and both routes reach it FIRST.
 * On the executive route it IS the chapter; on the technical route it is the
 * promise the room reads before it walks the six Stops (#92).
 *
 * IT IS THE STACK PARENT (Atil, 2026-08-28). The six Stop Slides hang under
 * this Slide, not under the chapter divider: `→` is the executive route on to
 * the payoff, `↓` is the technical route into the stack.
 *
 * WHAT IT IS: the Rail at full size, plus one plain line under each Stop
 * saying what the next Stop is judged against. That detail line is the whole
 * difference between comp A and comp F — A was seven names and a Headline the
 * wall did not support, and #91 had already ruled that a seven-across row
 * ORIENTS rather than ARGUES. The lines are what make this one argue.
 *
 * ══ LOCKED — do not relitigate ══
 *
 * NO SMALL RAIL. On this Slide the enlarged Rail and the small Rail are the
 * same object at two sizes, and drawing it twice is the contradiction #71 was
 * asked to answer.
 *
 * THE SEAM IS A MARK, NEVER A LABEL. The row breaks after `to-tickets` and the
 * ground after it changes weight. No words on it.
 *
 * THE COUNT IS SPOKEN AND NEVER PRINTED: "four of these seven happen before a
 * line of code exists", on the executive route only (#92). The room counts it
 * off the wall.
 *
 * NO FRAGMENTS AT ALL (Atil, 2026-08-26 — overriding the seven-Fragment walk
 * of 2026-08-20 and the one-press seam split of earlier the same day). The
 * Slide arrives whole: seven Stops, seven detail lines, every command, in the
 * first frame. The room reads the row across, which is what comp F was chosen
 * for, and the presenter speaks the count off a wall that is already complete.
 *
 * There is no Lit mark on this Slide — nothing walks, so nothing has a current
 * position. The Slide binds no key and spends nothing from the chapter's
 * widget budget.
 *
 * A GLYPH SITS ABOVE EVERY STOP NAME (Atil, 2026-08-28). Each one is a
 * miniature of that Stop's own Slide picture, so the technical route recognises
 * a shape it already walked and the executive route reads seven distinct marks
 * instead of seven words. The drawings live in `StopGlyph.astro` and carry no
 * text — the detail line under the name is what argues.
 *
 * ⚠ The small Rail still shows a Dim Stop's name and nothing else, because six
 * Slides each print their own Stop's commands. It prints the same `in progress`
 * marker on `Reconcile`, dimmed with the commands.
 *
 * `Reconcile` PRINTS `in progress` WHERE THE OTHER SIX PRINT A COMMAND (Atil,
 * 2026-08-28). This replaces the truly blank slot of #93: the room read the
 * blank as a missing line rather than as a deliberate gap. The marker is the
 * honest version — the two skills that Stop needs do not exist yet — and it is
 * drawn as a state, not as a command, so the row still shows six commands.
 *
 * TWO LINES OF ROOM FOR EVERY STOP NAME (#71), or a wrapping name drops its
 * detail line below the other six. `Reconcile` is one short word, so the
 * constraint is now slack rather than tight (#93) — keep it anyway.
 *
 * THE SLIDE IS IDENTICAL ON BOTH ROUTES. One behaviour, so the presenter never
 * has to remember which route they are in (#92).
 */
import { STOPS, SEAM } from "../../lib/asml-ai/pipeline";
import StopGlyph from "./StopGlyph.astro";

interface Props {
  /** Value for `data-section` — the on-screen section indicator. */
  section: string;
}

const { section } = Astro.props;
---

<section class="ch5-sum" data-section={section}>
  {/* The eyebrow is chapter 5's own gloss from roadmap.ts, word for word.
      Chapter 1's Roadmap promised "the process that repeats" thirty minutes
      earlier and the room has not seen it since; this is where it is paid off.
      Accepted knowingly (#92): the one place in the deck where a gloss is
      re-used, and no other chapter has a Slide that IS its own gloss. */}
  <span class="eyebrow">In a nutshell</span>
  {/* The verb is SETS, not writes (#92). The written-down-ness is carried by
      the detail lines and by the presenter, deliberately not by the Headline —
      do not quietly restore it. */}
  <h2 class="h-set">
    <span class="h-line"><span>Proposed WoW</span></span>
  </h2>
  <ol class="ch5-sum-rail" aria-label="The operating model — seven stops">
    {
      STOPS.map((s, i) => (
        <li
          class:list={[
            "ch5-stop",
            i > SEAM && "after-seam",
            i === SEAM + 1 && "first-after",
          ]}
        >
          <StopGlyph id={s.id} />
          <span class="ch5-stop-name">{s.name}</span>
          <span class="ch5-cmds">
            {s.cmds.map((c) => (
              <span class="ch5-cmd">{c}</span>
            ))}
            {/* `Reconcile` has no command, so it names its state in the same
                slot (Atil, 2026-08-28). The blank of #93 read as an oversight
                from the back of the room; `in progress` says the same thing on
                purpose. It is not styled as a command — see `.is-pending`. */}
            {s.pending && <span class="ch5-cmd is-pending">{s.pending}</span>}
          </span>
          <span class="ch5-judged">{s.judged}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>
  <slot />
</section>
